story “work better .” The bus driver is a true sadist; forests feel alive because
of unseen spirits; armies are dri ven to folly ē way lovers are. W e’ll
invent w hatever we õ in order ů our stories make se nse. Only if
stories m ake sense can we make  sense of our world. T elling stories may be
an essential existential act. W e tell stories to define our world in causal,
temporal terms we can ä. And maybe, at some level, we õ
tell ourselves stories to prove to ourselves that we exist.
Stories are told in simple jokes, oral sagas, ancient texts, live
performances and visual recordings. But stories aren’ t always transferable
across ç media. Just beca use ā · “told” or writ ten or acted
out in a dance or pantomime ¯ mean it lends itself to ğic telling.
Every day filmmakers start shorts and features that are misconceived and
