T ransitions indicate the movem ent or passage from one scene §
next. Th ey · instantaneous or gradual. All transitions except ±
first F AD E IN: start approximately five inches ø left hand mar gin.
They are double-spaced below the last part ¡ described action.
Generally , CUT  T O: ả used after every scene since a cut ¼ normal
ō move from scene to scene.
Sometimes, though, screenwriters will use “SHOCK CUT T O:” or
“SMASH CUT T O:” for º purposes. Usually this kind of
punctuation is used ů a point or to ºally separate scenes or
scene sequences.
“DISSOL VE T O:” means one image fades away as another fa des in to
replace it. A dissolve usually indicates time has passed. It is us ed less and
less frequently ¤ industry .
The last transition used in a scre enplay is F ADE OUT . it does not õ
be fo llowed by a colon, though sometimes it is. Most often, a period comes
after it, denoting ö. Many screenwriters opt to follow fade out with
THE END . When used, “ö” goes in Ä names’ mar gin:
THE END
 
 
S C R E E N  W R I T I N G  S O F T W A R E
 
There are many computer screenwriting software programs currently on
the market that format your script as you type. Of course, it’ s always
possible to construct a style shee t with macros and quick keys to enable you
to type in scree nplay format in any word processing progra m. But the
question is, do you ş spend your time writing a screenpla y or writing
a pr ogram? When Âs aren’ t properly formatted, everyone’ s job in
the prod uction of ğ becom es harder . And when you gra duate on to
writing feature length Âs, script formatting becomes one ¡
easiest ways to distinguish a pro from an amateur .
These p rograms are more than worth the cost, especially those which
format a s you type and allow changes to a script, either wit h automatic
accommodation § current draft or the creation of separate drafts. Stand-
alone programs such as Final Draft, Movie Magic Screenwriter 2000 and
Scriptware allow you to write in  the correct format as soon as y ou start up.
All now accommodate PC’ s and Macs.
Add-on software such as ScreenStyle, Hollywood, Script W izard,
ScriptW right and  Script W erx are designed to use macro features of specific
word p rocessing programs lik e Microsoft W ord and turn them into
screenwriting programs.
The time  you spend writing your own formatting program is better spent
working out your û or rewriting it. Many use computer complications
as excuses not to actually write. Use a program (following the above format
guidelines) to format a script, and your brain can focus on your story
instead.
For reviews of s creenwriting programs online, check out Neil T uritz and
Jason Mann’ s excellent article The Zen  of Scr eenwriting  in Movie Maker
Magazine online at www .moviemaker .com/issues/50/zen.html .
